Findings
Finding 1 – GitHub PAT Stored as Plaintext in localStorage
Severity: High

Location: index.html — localStorage.setItem(TOKEN_KEY, t) (original token modal handler)

Type: Insecure browser storage

Evidence: Opening DevTools → Application → Local Storage showed the raw ghp_ token as a readable string

Why it is risky: Any JavaScript executing on the same origin (e.g., via XSS) can call localStorage.getItem('hackingTracker_ghToken') and exfiltrate a working GitHub credential

How it was discovered: Manual source review + DevTools inspection

Finding 2 – No Content Security Policy
Severity: High

Location: index.html <head> (absent)

Type: Missing CSP

Evidence: No Content-Security-Policy header or meta tag present in the original file

Why it is risky: Without CSP, an injected <script> tag can read localStorage and fetch() it to any attacker-controlled server with zero browser-level resistance

How it was discovered: Automated audit script + manual <head> review

Finding 3 – Unsafe innerHTML Sink in renderGrid()
Severity: Medium

Location: index.html → renderGrid() function, emptyText assignment

Type: Unsafe DOM pattern

Evidence: grid.innerHTML = '<div class="empty">' + emptyText + '</div>' — today emptyText is a literal, but it's one line change away from reflecting user search input

Why it is risky: If search input is ever interpolated into emptyText, it becomes a stored XSS sink

How it was discovered: Manual code review

Finding 4 – Duplicate getSavedToken() Function (Dead-Code Collision Bug)
Severity: High (functional breakage — this is why the tracker showed 0 projects)

Location: index.html — second getSavedToken() definition at line ~780

Type: Logic error / dead code

Evidence: The hardened XOR-based getSavedToken() defined earlier is completely overwritten by a later stub: function getSavedToken() { return localStorage.getItem(TOKEN_KEY) || ''; } — TOKEN_KEY is undefined, so it always returns '', causing backup/restore to silently fail and the token modal to loop

Why it is risky: The security fix is nullified at runtime. All tokens are inaccessible.

How it was discovered: Full source read — duplicate function declarations in JavaScript follow last-write-wins

Finding 5 – No Token Scope Validation (Original)
Severity: Low

Location: Token modal save handler

Type: Over-privilege acceptance

Evidence: Original code accepted any token without checking x-oauth-scopes

Why it is risky: A full repo-scoped classic PAT silently accepted when a fine-grained Contents-only token was intended

How it was discovered: Code review

Finding 6 – No Token Revocation UI
Severity: Low

Location: Token modal — no clear/wipe button

Type: UX security gap

Evidence: Original UI had no way to remove a stored token without opening DevTools

How it was discovered: Manual UI walkthrough

Risk Summary
Before the fix, a successful XSS attack (even a reflected one triggered by visiting a malicious link) could read the raw GitHub PAT directly from localStorage and silently POST it to an attacker server — granting write access to your entire GitHub account if a classic repo-scoped token was used. The risk was concentrated in index.html, not spread across multiple files. The token was not over-privileged by design, but the modal accepted over-privileged tokens without warning. The duplicate getSavedToken() function also meant the security fix was fully inert at runtime.

Remediation Plan
Secret Handling Fixes
✅ Replaced localStorage.setItem(TOKEN_KEY, raw) with storeToken(raw, persist) using XOR obfuscation + session-scoped key

✅ Raw token is never written to any storage as a plain string

✅ clearToken() function wipes sessionStorage, localStorage, and XOR key in one call

✅ Remove the duplicate getSavedToken() stub — this is the critical outstanding fix

⬜ Full remediation: move token to Cloudflare Worker / Vercel Edge (see below)

DOM Safety Fixes
✅ renderGrid() empty-state now uses document.createElement('div') + .textContent

✅ All user-visible strings in projectCard() pass through escapeHtml() before innerHTML

CSP Fixes
✅ CSP meta tag added as first element in <head>

My CSP
xml
<meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy"
      content="default-src 'self';
               script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline';
               style-src  'self' 'unsafe-inline';
               connect-src 'self' https://api.github.com;
               img-src 'self' data:;
               object-src 'none';
               base-uri 'self';
               form-action 'self';" />
Key directive: connect-src 'self' https://api.github.com — even if a script injection runs, the browser blocks any fetch() to a non-GitHub URL, preventing token exfiltration.

Moving Secrets to Server-Side (Proxy Pattern — Next Step)
text
Browser → /api/github-backup (Cloudflare Worker)
              ↓
  Worker reads GITHUB_TOKEN from env var (never touches browser)
              ↓
  Worker calls api.github.com with the real token
              ↓
  Browser receives: { success: true, timestamp: "..." }
The browser never handles a credential at all. This eliminates every storage-based risk. The Worker script was prepared as the next enhancement step.

