What I Learned
localStorage is a global read target for any JavaScript on your origin — storing credentials there is equivalent to printing them on the page

XOR obfuscation is not encryption; it raises the bar for casual inspection but not for an attacker with script execution

A CSP connect-src directive is the real defense: it prevents exfiltration even after a successful script injection

JavaScript function declarations follow last-write-wins — a duplicate function stub anywhere in the file silently replaces the one above it

textContent is always safer than innerHTML for developer-controlled strings that might later incorporate user input

Browser DevTools → Application is the fastest way to verify that a storage security fix actually took effect

Fine-grained GitHub tokens scoped to one repo dramatically reduce blast radius vs. classic PATs

The only complete solution for browser-based credential handling is a server-side proxy — the browser should never hold a reusable secret

