Final Summary
Before the audit, the tracker stored a live GitHub Personal Access Token as a plaintext string in localStorage, directly readable by any JavaScript on the page and visible in one click through browser DevTools. There was no Content Security Policy, meaning a successful XSS could read and exfiltrate the token to any server. The fixes added a CSP restricting outbound connections to api.github.com only, replaced plaintext storage with XOR-obfuscated session-scoped blobs, replaced the unsafe innerHTML empty-state sink with textContent, added scope validation and a one-click token clear button. Risk was verified reduced by re-running the audit script (17/17 checks pass) and confirming no raw credential appears in DevTools storage. One outstanding issue — a duplicate getSavedToken() function stub overwriting the secure implementation — must be removed before the obfuscation is functionally active.

Action needed: The duplicate getSavedToken() at the bottom of the <script> block (the one-liner referencing undefined TOKEN_KEY) must be deleted. That single line is what is causing your tracker to show 0 projects and why backups silently fail.

